WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
What happens to a brain when the nucleus cannot catch up?

2
00:00:07.433 --> 00:00:11.193
Disrupt that machinery, and neurons can stall short of their layers.

3
00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:14.323
One result can be lissencephaly.

4
00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:17.423
The cortex has reduced or absent folding.

5
00:00:18.303 --> 00:00:21.243
In the classic form it is also thick and poorly layered.

6
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:25.807
Losing one copy of LIS1 is enough to cause it.

7
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.787
LIS1 is deleted in Miller-Dieker lissencephaly.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.510
That deletion also removes neighbouring genes, which contribute to a more severe

9
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:36.510
syndrome.

10
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:39.493
A mouse cortex is smooth to begin with.

11
00:00:39.993 --> 00:00:43.633
So in mice you look at migration speed and where neurons end up.

12
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:47.857
A second gene, doublecortin, sits on the X chromosome.

13
00:00:48.837 --> 00:00:51.757
Its protein binds and stabilizes microtubules.

14
00:00:52.777 --> 00:00:55.417
Boys with a mutation typically have lissencephaly.

15
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.580
Now a girl carries one mutant copy.

16
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.960
Each of her cells switches off one X at random.

17
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.200
What would her cortex look like?

18
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:14.993
She is a mosaic. Neurons using the normal copy migrate.

19
00:01:15.793 --> 00:01:17.393
Some using the mutant copy stall.

20
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:22.177
The stalled neurons form a band of grey matter beneath the cortex.

21
00:01:23.097 --> 00:01:24.457
It is called double cortex.

22
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.710
A kinase called Cdk5 sits upstream.

23
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.950
Its targets include doublecortin and a dynein regulator.

24
00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.630
Mice lacking it have disordered layers.

25
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:41.923
The two-stroke climb is not the whole trip.

26
00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:47.423
In embryonic rat cortex, newborn neurons first move to the subventricular zone.

27
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.963
There they become multipolar, with several short processes.

28
00:01:53.023 --> 00:01:57.503
They move slowly, change direction, and make little net progress for about a day or

29
00:01:57.503 --> 00:01:58.503
longer.

30
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:01.667
Many then move back toward the ventricle for a time.

31
00:02:02.507 --> 00:02:04.427
Why some cells go back is not settled.

32
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.970
Then they turn bipolar and begin the sustained climb to the cortical plate.

33
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.970
Now the front goes first.

34
00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:15.173
LIS1 matters at this turn too.

35
00:02:16.053 --> 00:02:18.733
So one defect can stall cells at more than one step.

36
00:02:23.117 --> 00:02:26.697
The two-stroke cycle explains one way migration can fail.

37
00:02:27.557 --> 00:02:30.857
When you film a migrating neuron, track the nucleus, not the tip.
